WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.600
- Welcome and hello, everyone.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.566
My name is Mira Debs and I
am the Executive Director

00:00:14.566 --> 00:00:17.820
of Yale's Education Studies program.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.230
We're so glad that you're here.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.190
The Yale Education Studies program

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.970
is an interdisciplinary
community that empowers students

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.150
and researchers to critically examine

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.890
and collectively reshape
the education landscape

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.453
through research, policy and practice.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.490
In our conversation this
evening, over the next hour,

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.300
we're gonna focus on the
role of student activism

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.570
and the media in addressing
educational injustice

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.470
and specifically, the
enduring school segregation

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:48.890
in New York City.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.040
The nation's largest school district,

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.240
home to 1.1 million students.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.670
New York City has an elaborate
school choice system,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.410
primarily for middle
and high school students

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.290
with a lot of schools that
have screened admissions

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:10.320
where student test scores,
student grades, GPAs, attendance,

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.548
sometimes, interviews and portfolios

00:01:14.548 --> 00:01:17.810
are all factored into
admissions decisions.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.690
There also is a gifted
and talented program.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.840
And there's a test for that starting

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.500
when students are four years old.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.440
So as a result of these screens,

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.520
an enduring problem in New York City

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:35.520
is that the most elite
schools are disproportionately

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.450
White and Asian in a district
that is majority black

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.563
and LatinX students.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.510
And so as you'll learn this evening,

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.280
students have been
fighting for over 50 years

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.660
to integrate New York City schools.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:54.000
In 1964, the largest
civil rights demonstration

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.137
during the civil rights movement,

00:01:55.137 --> 00:01:59.320
464,000 students participated

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.410
in a walkout protesting
the segregated schools

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.110
of New York City.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.840
And in the last decade, the
topic of school integration

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.510
with the help of student
activists from organizations

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.480
including Teens Take Charge

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.470
and Integrate New York
City have been integral

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.950
in pushing this issue back

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.073
to the center of the education agenda.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:23.180
The students have also focused
not only on integration

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.209
but educational self-determination

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:29.680
and empowering students
to determine alongside

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.123
with their communities,
an equitable future.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.090
You may be wondering why focus

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.660
on school integration right now?

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.970
We're in the middle of a pandemic.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:44.550
Many schools are closed,
students are out of school.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.453
So why this issue right now?

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.330
As I've heard repeatedly
from students and parents,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:52.970
and I'm currently doing research

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:57.970
with Molly Makris and Elise
Castillo on this topic,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.603
this issue is only more urgent right now.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.340
The pandemic has made
inequality between neighborhoods

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.300
and between communities and
schools even more apparent.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:13.740
There is no going back
to the way things were.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:15.520
And in the last two months,

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.430
there has been some significant progress

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.490
in terms of announcements from the mayor

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:24.090
and the school's chancellor
regarding middle school screens,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.440
some high school admissions

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.750
and it changed to the
gifted and talented test.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:31.290
So there's momentum and
change happening right now

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.940
even in the middle of a pandemic.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.163
A few notes on logistics
before we get started.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.060
Our panel discussion is
gonna run about 35 minutes

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.960
and then we will have time for questions.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.540
And you can submit a question at any time

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:48.563
via the Q&amp;A tab in the
bottom of your screen.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:55.360
And it is now my honor to pass
the baton to our moderator,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.570
Sarah Medina Camiscoli.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.950
Sarah is a third year law student at Yale.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:04.470
She's a lecture in the
Education Studies program.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.270
And she's also one of the adult founders

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.060
of Integrate New York City.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.720
And for that reason, Sarah
is a living representation

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.220
of the nexus of research
policy and practice

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.380
which is core to the ed studies program.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.053
So thank you so much, Sarah.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.350
- Yeah, thank you, Mira.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.720
Hey, all, this is such a gift
to be with you all today.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.162
I'd like to kick us off as we tend to do

00:04:27.162 --> 00:04:31.730
in the youth organizing space
with setting some intentions.

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.650
And so the intentions
I've set to for today

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.270
and gotten consent from our panelists for,

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:41.350
are one, to create an
intergenerational discussion

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:44.610
on self-determined movement,
building and resistance

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.230
for integration and structural equity.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.880
So what does that mean
for you in the audience

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.940
and what does that mean for our panelists?

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.090
It means that I ask that you listen deeply

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.730
and write questions, comments, ideas,

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.610
