00:09:47.828 --> 00:09:50.340
The first question is for Mr. Delmont.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.090
Take us back to the New York City activism

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:56.390
that you cover in your
book, Why Busing Failed.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:58.900
What were the demonstrations of students

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:02.123
and parents and how were
they covered by the media?

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:04.890
- Thanks, Sarah for that question.

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:06.300
So as professor Debs mentioned,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.347
there was a massive
school boycott in 1964.

00:10:08.347 --> 00:10:10.910
The largest protest in the
history of civil rights.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.570
460,000 students stay out of school

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:15.997
to protest a school
starvation in New York City.

00:10:15.997 --> 00:10:17.380
And what's interesting about the story

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:18.990
in New York is it really had been going on

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:20.380
for a whole decade prior to that.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.680
So usually when we were
taught about the history

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.400
of school segregation
and school integration,

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.850
it's usually a southern
story, but the story

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:29.150
of New York City is really
a parallel story to that,

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:30.330
to the southern story.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.770
So in the early 1950s, Ella Baker,

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.860
the famous civil rights
organizer is the president

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.600
of the New York branch of the NAACP.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:39.800
And she's working with Kenneth Clark,

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.590
the famous psychologist
to organize parents

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:45.380
and civic leaders to really protest

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:46.970
to kind of extreme inequality

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.260
they see in the city's public schools.

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.110
They know that black and LatinX students

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:52.570
are going to schools
that are under-resourced.

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:55.080
They know that a lot of these
textbooks in those schools

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.800
have racist texts and racist images.

00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.820
And they want the students
be able to go to schools

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.800
that have equal resources
and are equitable.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.380
That happens even before
the Brown vs Board decision.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.480
When Brown vs Board is decided
by the Supreme Court in 1954,

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.106
activists in New York say, why not here?

00:11:12.106 --> 00:11:14.590
The decision is focused
on southern schools

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:16.580
but they see, they look all
around and they see that schools

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:19.340
in New York are equally
segregated and they demand action.

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:23.240
They demand action
through public protests,

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:24.800
through editorials and through statements

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:25.940
at school board meetings.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:27.940
'Cause they're trying to bring
this issue into the public.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:29.720
For a lot of folks in
New York at that time,

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.810
they just didn't believe
that school segregation,

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.360
educational inequality
could be issues in New York.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.470
They thought this is a
liberal progressive place.

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:38.200
These kind of issues
only happen in the south.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:39.640
They can actually happen in New York City.

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.600
So they're trying to push out in the open.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.210
In 1957, the same year

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.410
as the Little Rock school
integration crisis,

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:47.730
there's a group of mothers in
New York who filed a lawsuit

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.280
against the school board to
protest educational inequality.

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:51.240
And they hold their children

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.140
out of school for months in protest.

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.420
This is a boycott that risked jail time.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.850
And the black press dubbed
them the Harlem Nine.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.390
So to play on the Little Rock Nine.

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:00.780
So this is going on at the exact same time

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:02.350
what's going on in the south.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.220
These protests continue and escalate

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:06.440
in the late 1950s, early 1960s.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:07.437
Reverend Milton Galamison

00:12:07.437 --> 00:12:08.940
and then a woman named Annie Stein.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.630
We had a group called the
Parents' Workshop for Equality.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:15.200
And they threatened as early
as 1960 a large-scale boycott

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:16.770
because they're just
calling on the school board

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:20.300
to do something meaningful to
address school segregation.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.140
Over this entire period from 1954 to 1964,

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:25.550
the school board makes
only really small changes.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:27.370
So they might move a few hundred students

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:28.930
from an overcrowded school in the Bronx

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:31.740
to a bus crowded school in Queens.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:33.620
But mostly, the school
board drags their feet.

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:34.760
They refuse to acknowledge

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:36.170
that school segregations are a real issue.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.420
They refuse to take any meaningful steps.

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:40.520
And even those small scale changes are met

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.240
with intense resistance
from white parents.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:44.910
Who frankly, wanna
maintain the status quo.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:47.890
They wanna maintain racial
segregation in the schools.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:49.600
So by February, 1964,

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.040
when this massive school
boycott takes place,

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.140
civil rights activists have
been fighting the school board

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:55.270
for more than a decade

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:56.320
and they have a little to show for it.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:59.300
So they're exhausted, they're
frustrated and they demanded

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:02.140
this is the time that action's
finally gonna take place.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:04.690
That boycott itself brings
together a coalition

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:05.580
of different groups,

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:08.970
both kind of more moderate
groups, more militant groups.

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.740
It's something that
doesn't really get taught

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:14.090
in the same way in our textbooks,

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:15.157
because the media at the time,

00:13:15.157 --> 00:13:16.420
particularly the White media,

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:17.960
seems like the New York Times

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:19.430
disregards that kind of protest.

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:24.430
They call it a violent active
adult-encouraged truancy.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.140
And so it's not treated with the same

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.540
kind of sense of urgency
as a civil rights protests

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.410
going on in Montgomery,
Selma and Little Rock

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:31.280
at the same time.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:32.660
And so I think for me, what's important

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:35.527
about that story about school integration

00:13:35.527 --> 00:13:38.170
and the civil rights
activism in New York City

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:41.900
is that parents, students
were intensely organized.

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:43.370
And they're trying to
fight to make this an issue

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:46.180
that would be acknowledged,
but the school board

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.800
at every turn, tried to push back

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:49.950
and maintain the status
quo of segregation.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:55.783
- Thanks, Matt.

00:13:55.783 --> 00:13:56.750
I have another question here.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:58.530
And then folks, I'm
gonna be turning it over

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.730
to our student activists here

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.900
on the panel to ask any
follow up questions.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.360
And if not, I'll probably jump in

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:06.870
to some questions that
I'm seeing in the chat.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:10.610
But what did people understand the story

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:12.850
to be about desegregation?

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:15.070
And what would you say
is the most important

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:19.050
misunderstanding that you
wanted to disrupt in your book?

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:20.310
- Yeah, one of the
things that's fascinating

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:22.640
to me about this history in the north

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.300
is there's a sense that it was optional.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:27.080
So sometimes, people will
talk about the history

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.220
of segregation in the north
and say it was de facto.

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:31.790
That it was somehow by
customer, by market forces.

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:33.790
And this is segregation,
school segregation

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.590
in places like New York, Chicago, Boston,

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:37.260
it was intentional.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.940
The school boards drew zoning lines

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.150
in ways that would
purposely create schools

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:42.820
that were predominantly Latino,

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.550
predominantly white,
predominantly African-American.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:47.450
But they did it in such a
way that they kind of hid

00:14:47.450 --> 00:14:50.260
the racist intent of it, which
made it very, very difficult

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.387
for activists to bring
these cases into court

00:14:52.387 --> 00:14:55.080
and to actually get justice and to finally

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.790
get some actual
integration in the schools.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:59.900
So, one thing I tried to show in the book

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:03.100
was that these protests
over school desegregation,

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:04.690
they really needed to,
when we think about them

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:06.430
in the north, we need to think about them

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:08.230
with the same sense of moral urgency

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:11.050
as we tend to think about
school segregation in the south.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:14.830
That over the years, the
debate has really been switched

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:16.940
in such a way that all
of the focus becomes

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:19.960
on the perspectives and
desires of white parents.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:21.360
Whereas when we're talking
about school integration,

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:22.930
what we should be
focusing on is the rights

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.933
and educational outcomes for
black and LatinX students.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:30.420
- Thanks, Matt.

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:33.980
Do any of our student activists
here have any questions?

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:35.460
I feel like Matt in particular,

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:39.990
brought up some of the untold
stories of mostly black

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:43.073
and Puerto Rican student
organizers in New York City.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:46.583
Anyone wanna ask any questions?

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:55.050
I had a question Matt, that
I wanted to follow up on,

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:58.800
why do you feel like it's
often such an untold story

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:01.540
that the largest civil
rights demonstration

00:16:02.882 --> 00:16:04.360
was a half million black

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:06.480
and Puerto Rican students in New York?

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.230
What do you think is driving that story

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:12.700
not being covered and not
being made front and center

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:15.510
at the time when it happened and today?

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.380
Because I'm sure a lot
of people on this call

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:20.200
are like, wait a minute,
let me fact check that.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:22.490
The largest civil rights demonstration

00:16:22.490 --> 00:16:24.073
was Black and Puerto Rican
students in New York City?

00:16:24.073 --> 00:16:25.963
I don't think that's right.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.350
- Yeah, and the reason didn't
get attention at the time.

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:31.180
And the reason it hasn't
gotten much attention

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:34.610
until recently is that
it's a more uncomfortable

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:36.140
and difficult story for a lot of people,

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:38.850
particularly folks in
the north to reckon with.

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:42.690
What I mean by that is it was
easier for white folks in 1964

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:45.310
whether that's the parents,
the school board or media

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:47.028
to point to places in the south and say,

00:16:47.028 --> 00:16:48.780
bad things that are
happening in the south.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:49.820
That's where racism exists.

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:52.430
It exists down in Montgomery
and down in Selma.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.563
It can't possibly exist in New York.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:58.940
That's to actually
think about the history.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:00.940
Honestly, it forces people to reckon

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:03.271
with the privilege they have
in their own neighborhoods

00:17:03.271 --> 00:17:05.477
in terms of housing, in terms of schools

00:17:05.477 --> 00:17:07.970
and it actually forces
them to give up something.

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:09.500
So one of the things
that Martin Luther King

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.690
would always say when he came
to speak in New York City

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:14.310
and other places in New
York was that white liberals

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:16.850
and white moderates had to
actually live up to their ideals.

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:18.840
It wasn't enough to be
liberal when we're talking

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.130
about race just in the south,

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:21.320
you had to be a liberal and actually came

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:23.510
to your own neighborhood,
into your own backyard.

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:25.073
I mean, I think that was true in the 60s,

00:17:25.073 --> 00:17:25.906
I think it's true today.

00:17:25.906 --> 00:17:26.930
It's hard.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:28.830
There's a willful ignorance
that a lot of people

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.590
would like to have about the realities

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:31.813
of racism in our country.

00:17:31.813 --> 00:17:32.750
I think that's particularly true

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:34.110
in places that people want to think of

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.577
as being somehow more
progressive or more liberal.

00:17:36.577 --> 00:17:40.820
It allows them to not deal
with the actual reality

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:42.973
of how race is structured in our country.

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.520
- I actually have one
follow-up question for you

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:51.130
and then I'm gonna pass it
along to the folks here.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:52.962
I heard you say asking folks to be liberal

00:17:52.962 --> 00:17:54.920
in their own neighborhoods.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:55.880
It almost sounds to me

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.120
like the word liberal and anti-racist

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.890
are being interchangeable
in that statement.

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.630
Could you maybe elaborate
a little bit more for me

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:06.460
around how you understand conservativism

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:09.130
versus liberalism in this conversation?

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:12.100
And when they do and don't become words

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:14.540
that can be replaced with one another?

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:16.430
- Sure, that's a big question.

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:18.080
So thinking about the
time period I'm studying,

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:20.040
the circle time period.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:22.740
Conservatives would have
been more directly outspoken

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.000
and say, I'm thinking of
folks like Barry Goldwater,

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:26.050
the Senator from Arizona

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:28.830
who was explicitly opposed
to the Civil Rights Act.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.280
He thought it was unconstitutional.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:33.180
He didn't think that the
government had a role to play

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:35.060
in promoting integration.

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:36.920
So I think conservatives
in that time period

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:38.960
were more vocal in their opposition.

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:42.070
Liberals, white liberals
in this case had a position

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.210
where they would say they're in favor

00:18:43.210 --> 00:18:45.910
of integration generally,
but when push came to shove,

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:47.890
they did everything they could
to not actually integrate

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:49.630
their own neighborhoods
and their own schools.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:54.630
And so I think that's a sort
of a structuring reality

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:58.580
about how white liberalism
has functioned in our country

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:01.830
is that it's liberal in
sort of general strokes,

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.510
but not actually in
practice when actually comes

00:19:04.510 --> 00:19:07.070
to things that directly
impact people's lives.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:09.750
The term anti-racist
didn't exist in the 60s.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:11.670
But I think part of what
King was calling for

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:14.230
was people to be proactive,
to take the actions

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:17.107
that would follow up on their ideals.

00:19:17.107 --> 00:19:20.230
And so I think to transpose
it into the present,

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:22.210
I think the kind of
things we see taking place

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:25.470
in New York City right now,
it doesn't do much good to say

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:27.610
you're in favor of educational equality

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:30.700
if you're not actually going
to support, proactively support

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:32.809
the policies and the steps
that are gonna produce

00:19:32.809 --> 00:19:35.226
those outcomes on the ground.

00:19:37.217 --> 00:19:39.000
- Thanks so much, folks.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.240
I'm gonna move to our Integrated NYC

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:44.320
and Teens Take Charge folks.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:46.170
But wanna give folks here an opportunity

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:50.323
to possibly ask any questions,
any follow up to Matt.

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:55.400
Okay.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:56.930
I also just wanna shout out.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:20:00.680
I see a lot of folks in the
chat from integrated schools.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:02.993
Such a blessing to be with you all today.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:07.540
So I wanna first start off,

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:10.710
Matt talks a lot in his book about busing

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:14.430
and how busing becomes the
thing that people talk about

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:17.510
is segregation and is the
issue around integration.

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:20.890
And that's the real issue and
why it's remained segregated.

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:22.460
I'm wondering if we could start maybe

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:27.130
with IntegrateNYC first and
talking about in 2014 and 2015,

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:33.110
when y'all first started, what
was the busing of the time?

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:37.193
What was the thing that people
told you integration was?

00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:40.390
And what were the ways
that you had to really

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:43.713
push against that to create the five Rs?

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.840
- I think busing was the thing for us.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:56.300
Like maybe not busing busing,

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:58.264
but just like the movement of bodies.

00:20:58.264 --> 00:21:00.940
And maybe like enrollment
was only talked about

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:02.500
when we first started

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:05.820
and the quick solution
was sort of seen as,

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:09.260
well, if we just make the
schools look different,

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:10.670
that will solve the problem

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:13.620
or if we make them look desegregated,

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:14.940
that will solve the problem.

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:19.370
But I don't think the
people making this policy,

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:20.203
it's not I don't think,

00:21:20.203 --> 00:21:23.340
I know that the people making
this policy didn't think

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:27.200
about what students felt and
experienced every single day

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.730
because us students, it
was so obvious for us

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:33.050
to see that enrollment
wasn't the only issue

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:36.721
and enrollment wasn't
the only solution either.

00:21:36.721 --> 00:21:40.470
And so that's why when we
began our youth councils

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:44.780
and meeting with students
all across the five boroughs,

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:47.340
we created the five Rs.

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:50.930
We will be first like talked
about our experiences.

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:53.470
And I saw somebody asked like
what a school exchange is.

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:57.670
And for us, it's basically
like when you buddy up

00:21:57.670 --> 00:21:58.840
with someone in the other school,

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:01.551
you have a group of students
kind of buddying each other up

00:22:01.551 --> 00:22:05.108
and you take each other through
your day in your school.

00:22:05.108 --> 00:22:07.061
And for all of us, that community

00:22:07.061 --> 00:22:09.740
and relationship work was really important

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:12.010
because it allowed us to
see each other's experience

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:14.426
and then have similar conversations

00:22:14.426 --> 00:22:17.420
with other students from
other schools around the city.

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:19.800
And that's kind of how we came

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:23.050
about our Five R platform,
which is race in enrollment.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:26.180
So yes, let's address race in enrollment

00:22:26.180 --> 00:22:28.120
and actually name like race.

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:30.713
And what are other
factors that we are taking

00:22:30.713 --> 00:22:32.870
into account when enrolling students?

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:34.580
Because that shouldn't be the only thing.

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:37.040
And like, how do we use those factors

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:39.950
to benefit students rather
than like penalize them

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:42.330
for stuff that has
happened in their lives.

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:45.310
We have resources which talks

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:49.440
about how resources are are allocated

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:51.280
inequitably across like schools.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:53.540
Some schools get so many sports scenes,

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:54.700
other schools get to zero.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:56.880
Some schools get three science labs,

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:58.973
others get broken lab equipment.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.860
And we talk about
representation, which talks about

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:07.460
how, which really addresses
a lot of our teachers

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.740
and a lot of the people
who are teaching us stuff

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:14.180
and like in front of
the classroom are white

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:17.123
and don't look like most
Black and Brown students.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.903
And don't really, are not able to identify

00:23:20.903 --> 00:23:22.586
and connect with them in that way.

00:23:22.586 --> 00:23:26.560
So how are we addressing
that issue as well.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:28.990
We talk about relationships,
which is like, okay,

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:30.440
once we're all in the building

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:34.407
and we're in a diversified
space, what does that mean?

00:23:34.407 --> 00:23:37.010
How do we build relationships together?

00:23:37.010 --> 00:23:40.030
Like how, we're not just
gonna ignore all the tension

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:43.206
and the history of
trauma that our families,

00:23:43.206 --> 00:23:44.657
our individual families

00:23:44.657 --> 00:23:47.490
and our individual
bodies have gone through.

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:50.043
And we also have
restorative justice which,

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:52.430
so lost, (indistinct).

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:55.040
(laughs), these Five Rs.

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:57.870
I also wanna invite Obrian
if you wanna add anything

00:23:57.870 --> 00:23:58.970
in those Rs.

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:00.860
Our last R is restorative justice,

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:03.860
which deals with the
schooler prism pipeline

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:06.630
which shows that a lot of our students

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.280
and especially our
Black and Brown students

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:10.893
are being locked up and
are being isolated from,

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:14.430
may perhaps the only
institution that could give them

00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:16.923
any kind of stability.

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:20.830
And so, what does restorative
justice look like?

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:24.650
And how do we really build a community

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:26.580
where we can learn from our mistakes

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:27.840
and not punish students,

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:30.593
especially our Black and Brown students.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:34.680
- Thanks so much, Aneth.

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.930
I'd love to hear a little
bit from Teens Take Charge

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:41.220
about the way that you've disrupted media

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:44.120
and the way that you've used new media,

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:46.280
new ways to address media,

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:48.417
the way that the podcast has done that

00:24:48.417 --> 00:24:51.980
and really shifted the
way that young people

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:54.520
are like Matt was saying, push to the side

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:56.920
in the narrative about integration.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:25:00.010
What are the innovative ways
that you've really transformed

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:03.363
how students are centered
in mainstream media?

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:06.210
- Yeah, I think Matt brings up

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:09.810
a really good point when
talking about how the north

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:12.480
was kind of seen as this like holy grail.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:14.601
And to this day, it's like very much.

00:25:14.601 --> 00:25:18.040
We see a lot of, dare I
say, like neocolonization

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:23.040
of terms like progressive
and democrat or liberal.

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:24.993
And what we find is a lot of parents,

00:25:24.993 --> 00:25:28.300
especially parents were
opposed to integration

00:25:28.300 --> 00:25:31.290
kind of use these as almost
shields to protect them

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:33.277
from their own racist tendencies

00:25:33.277 --> 00:25:35.830
when talking about education.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:38.320
And so what Teens Take Charge
has been doing a lot of,

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:41.620
it's trying to infiltrate as
much as the media as possible

00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:43.600
to kind of make these problems known.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:46.900
It's been swept under the rug
for literally decades, right?

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:50.960
And we know that if there's
anything that our generation

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:53.650
can do, is completely
infiltrate the media.

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:57.530
And so, we made sure to
center as much student voices

00:25:57.530 --> 00:25:59.530
as possible because
those are undisputable,

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:00.520
at least I'd hope to be.

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:05.450
And so with the podcast,
we're talking about

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:08.720
the in-person experiences
that different students have

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:11.610
at different underfunded
institutions and things like that.

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:12.750
That's one way.

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:15.570
We've been so fortunate to have plenty,

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:18.695
like a couple of documentaries
about student experiences

00:26:18.695 --> 00:26:22.570
and their different
intersectional experiences

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:27.090
about living in a extremely
segregated school system.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:30.893
But also just like things
like Instagram and Twitter,

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:35.500
that has also been really
fundamental at educating

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:39.760
our followers and reaching
all over the place

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:42.640
because I mean, it's everywhere.

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.250
How can we engage with our audience

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:46.080
in a more personal level?

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:49.520
And when we do these
types of media campaigns,

00:26:49.520 --> 00:26:51.513
hashtags and things like that as well.

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:57.450
- Yeah, wow.

00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:59.470
Every time I hear what the
media, I just get really excited.

00:26:59.470 --> 00:27:02.630
As in take charges,
former co-director for us

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:05.700
because that was my life from
the year that I was involved

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:07.860
in an organization before graduating.

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:10.680
And I feel like in my
experience, adding on

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:13.250
to what Alex said, definitely the media

00:27:13.250 --> 00:27:15.320
has been prevalent in
steering the conversation

00:27:15.320 --> 00:27:16.340
and bringing light to it.

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:20.800
But I feel like at least when
I published my first op ed,

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:22.670
it was a really like personal experience.

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:27.330
I remember walking into school
the next day and my principal

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:29.340
stopping me in the hallway
and asking me about

00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:32.400
school integration in my
work and like praising my,

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.860
and she shared it with other teachers

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:35.930
and students within the school.

00:27:35.930 --> 00:27:37.910
And that started a school-wide discussion

00:27:37.910 --> 00:27:40.560
about school integration
for around a week or so.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:42.090
Like every time I publish something,

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:43.633
it was featured in something.

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:45.830
Students and teachers together,

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:47.790
we kind of just learned from
each other's experiences

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:50.830
when we had the free
time to engage in that.

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:52.900
So storytelling is definitely a big part

00:27:52.900 --> 00:27:55.310
of youth organizing
around school integration.

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:59.348
And it really does start conversations

00:27:59.348 --> 00:28:01.470
and allows people to like
change the narratives

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:05.880
and come together and just
create a new understanding

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:09.023
and vision for education
in the future, so, yeah.

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:11.810
- Thank you so much.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.000
I have a conversation.

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:15.440
I've appreciated a couple
of comments in the chat

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:18.150
because oftentimes, what
people are looking at

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:19.980
is what's in the media

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:22.430
but not knowing about what's
actually in the movement

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:25.370
and the way that we have
to work to heal each other

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:26.980
and support each other.

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:28.850
And so I wanted to get more

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:32.290
on a personal level to
really give folks here,

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:35.620
this is a form of media arguably, right?

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:38.760
The opportunity to just really appreciate

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:41.420
what the folks here are doing

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:44.250
and the many lonely challenging moments

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:46.230
outside of the exciting meetings

00:28:46.230 --> 00:28:48.810
or the inspiring time at Forever 21.

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:51.630
There's a lot of lonely
times when you wonder,

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:53.370
do I wanna take that risk?

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:54.871
Do I wanna deal with
people on the internet

00:28:54.871 --> 00:28:57.140
making racial slurs at me?

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:58.770
Do I wanna deal with my classmates

00:28:58.770 --> 00:29:00.660
or parents putting me down?

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:02.850
And so I'd like to ask
this first question.

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:05.407
It comes from one of
our students who said,

00:29:05.407 --> 00:29:08.670
"What is a moment that
you felt segregation

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:12.280
in your body that you
actually felt the experience?

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:13.770
What did it feel like?

00:29:13.770 --> 00:29:15.630
Where were you and would you be willing

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:17.807
to share a little bit
with the group here?"

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:25.280
- I can go first.

00:29:25.280 --> 00:29:27.530
So I went to a specialized high school.

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:30.280
I went to Brooklyn Technical High School.

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:33.383
And you know, that school is like,

00:29:34.407 --> 00:29:36.710
"The most diverse
specialized high school."

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:39.381
But the representation
for students like myself

00:29:39.381 --> 00:29:43.640
as an Afro-Latino is
like almost zero to none.

00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:46.280
So I think that it's common at least,

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:50.198
for Black students, for LatinX students

00:29:50.198 --> 00:29:54.440
to gather in different
meetings and different spaces.

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:56.290
So for instance, there's like the BSU,

00:29:56.290 --> 00:29:58.010
which is the Black Student Union.

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:01.020
As well as ASPIRA, which is this club

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:05.460
that's "An investment in
Latino youth and LatinX youth."

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:08.600
And I think that it's a very alienating,

00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:11.210
it's a very like lonely space to navigate

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:12.980
especially in a specialized high school

00:30:12.980 --> 00:30:15.140
with more than like 6,000 plus students.

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:18.500
And where you only see
a very small percentage

00:30:18.500 --> 00:30:21.130
of that 6,000 student mass

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:22.883
being students that look like you.

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:28.350
to add onto that, I
don't think I can recall

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.400
or I can count with the fingers on my hand

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:32.297
how many teachers I've had
that were teachers of color,

00:30:32.297 --> 00:30:34.200
were teachers that looked like me.

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:37.640
And all of that speaks to the different Rs

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:38.610
and the different platforms

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:40.307
that we work with at IntegrateNYC.

00:30:40.307 --> 00:30:45.290
But I feel like, I also had
privileges when it came to that.

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:47.440
So I went to a New York City
specialized high school.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:49.900
So I had access to all the different APs.

00:30:49.900 --> 00:30:51.930
I had access to 40 plus sports teams.

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:56.930
I had access to a whole
freaking plane flight navigator

00:30:57.440 --> 00:30:58.980
on like in the school building.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:00.873
And to know that there's students

00:31:00.873 --> 00:31:03.210
that don't even have a cafeteria to sit

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:04.970
and have lunch at, there are students

00:31:04.970 --> 00:31:06.142
that don't have any
access to sports scenes,

00:31:06.142 --> 00:31:08.626
there are students that, you know,

00:31:08.626 --> 00:31:09.883
there's houseless students,

00:31:09.883 --> 00:31:11.800
there's students in
the foster care system.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:13.530
And like there's people that are coming

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:15.330
from all these different backgrounds

00:31:16.245 --> 00:31:17.330
and to step into a building

00:31:17.330 --> 00:31:18.860
where you're already not really welcomed,

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:21.370
you're not really represented
in the curriculum.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:23.300
I feel like it's something
that we all deal with.

00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:26.630
But for me personally, it's like

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:29.960
I go to Howard University now
where I have Black educators

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:32.040
and I learn about my history
and about black history

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:33.847
but I would have never done that

00:31:33.847 --> 00:31:35.750
if it would have been in the New York City

00:31:35.750 --> 00:31:37.030
public school system

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:39.610
I would have never had access
to these types of things.

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:41.910
And I never really had people
that looked like me around me.

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:44.310
So I was always kind of on my own.

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:47.680
And that's like, there's
several different instances.

00:31:47.680 --> 00:31:49.719
I can just name one where I just felt

00:31:49.719 --> 00:31:53.090
the segregation present, but it's real

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:55.814
and it exists and I was going through that

00:31:55.814 --> 00:31:56.980
and I didn't realize that
I was going through that

00:31:56.980 --> 00:31:59.683
at the time until I started
working with IntegrateNYC.

00:32:01.381 --> 00:32:03.510
- Obrian, thanks so much for sharing that.

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:05.330
I really heard in his sharing

00:32:05.330 --> 00:32:07.540
about the experience of loneliness

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:12.540
and the experience of
just really feeling torn

00:32:13.470 --> 00:32:15.550
between trying to get an opportunity

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:17.260
and trying to support your community.

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:19.280
And I'm wondering if anyone else here

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.120
on the panel has felt things

00:32:21.120 --> 00:32:22.930
like loneliness or feeling torn?

00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:26.220
Are there other feelings
that are present for you

00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:30.500
as young people who have
lived through segregation

00:32:30.500 --> 00:32:33.460
and lived through this largest
segregated school system

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:34.873
in the United States?

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:44.170
- Yeah, I mean, like Obrian was sharing,

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:47.050
it's almost hard to kind of
just pinpoint one experience

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:49.700
because I think we grow
up in these school systems

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:51.798
and we kinda get so used to being

00:32:51.798 --> 00:32:56.798
in these very kind of
microcosmic environments

00:32:56.900 --> 00:33:00.060
that you don't really
necessarily recognize

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:02.780
hey, I'm living in a
segregated school system.

00:33:02.780 --> 00:33:05.630
Especially if what you're
learning in school,

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:08.870
in history always puts away the focus

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:13.450
from New York City and your
experiences onto the south.

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:16.551
It almost serves as kind of
like this crazy scapegoat

00:33:16.551 --> 00:33:21.140
that prevents often, the white
history teachers from looking

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:24.240
into themselves in way that
they also segregate students

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:25.963
within their own classrooms.

00:33:27.555 --> 00:33:29.850
And I guess one thing that I can share

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:32.000
is I think my school,
I went to predominantly

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:35.160
Black and Brown (indistinct)
school in Brooklyn.

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:38.130
And one of the things
that my school tried to do

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:41.020
to give access to a lot of resources

00:33:41.020 --> 00:33:42.720
to its students is kind of send them

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:45.544
on enrichment programs
outside of the school.

00:33:45.544 --> 00:33:47.779
So kind of going to
different parts of the city

00:33:47.779 --> 00:33:51.330
and engaging in different
types of activities

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:53.430
and extracurriculars that the school

00:33:53.430 --> 00:33:55.970
just couldn't offer in-house.

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:59.390
And a lot of the times,
they would send the students

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:03.430
into very, very white and wealthy spaces.

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:06.260
And one that comes to
mind is a model congress

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:09.820
that I went to around the 10th grade.

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:12.285
And it was probably one of
the most jarring experiences

00:34:12.285 --> 00:34:14.250
that I've ever really had

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:16.960
because I didn't really know
there were white students

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:19.143
in New York City for a really long time.

00:34:20.180 --> 00:34:22.700
Because I grew up in the little Caribbean.

00:34:22.700 --> 00:34:26.310
I went to Black and Brown
schools pretty much all my life.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:30.030
And so to then go to a model congress,

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:32.503
which is very like a showboaty type thing,

00:34:34.031 --> 00:34:37.905
and then to see that like
out of like, I don't know,

00:34:37.905 --> 00:34:38.738
200, 300 students, my school's
the only delegation composed

00:34:41.494 --> 00:34:43.380
of entirely students of color.

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.720
It was the most jarring thing to ever see.

00:34:45.720 --> 00:34:48.610
And I was often the only person of color

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:50.743
within just a room.

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:54.850
And that is when I started to get hit

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:56.610
with all these classic experiences.

00:34:56.610 --> 00:34:59.430
I'm sure a lot of people here would relate

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:01.850
to the microaggressions.

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:04.223
Even incidents of just overt racism.

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:10.000
Very gatekeeper, ask language
and things like that.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:12.280
And so I think from
there on now, I started

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:16.350
to become way more cognizant
of just all the experiences.

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:19.700
It kind of started to hit me
like a lot at the same time.

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:21.850
And it took me some time to also just heal

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:23.700
from that trauma that I had to endure

00:35:24.831 --> 00:35:27.823
in conferences like
that from there on out.

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:39.860
- Yeah, I think it's a
really interesting question.

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:42.300
I think like we're constantly living

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:44.180
in a segregated society, right?

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:47.580
So even if some of us have
graduated high school,

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:52.580
I think all of us have,
we still stepped into,

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:57.590
most of us are like
stepping into segregated

00:35:57.590 --> 00:35:59.026
higher education institutions.

00:35:59.026 --> 00:36:02.480
And even outside education institutions,

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:07.480
we do live in a segregated space I guess.

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:12.320
And it is challenging.

00:36:12.320 --> 00:36:16.780
I feel like throughout the time,

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:19.426
and throughout our work, we've gained

00:36:19.426 --> 00:36:23.000
the language to maybe tell our story

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:24.877
and be like, okay, this is happening,

00:36:24.877 --> 00:36:27.890
and like kinda name that.

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:30.353
And that has in itself, been hard

00:36:30.353 --> 00:36:33.187
like learning how to name your story

00:36:33.187 --> 00:36:37.230
and how to tell it it's a real process.

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:42.230
And I think, we also feel like
a lot of imposter syndrome,

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:44.700
even in our organizing
spaces, I feel like maybe

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:48.510
that's the loneliest I've ever felt.

00:36:48.510 --> 00:36:51.050
When imposter syndrome
kind of starts creeping in.

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:53.560
And there's not like one
time that had happened

00:36:53.560 --> 00:36:54.393
and then that's it.

00:36:54.393 --> 00:36:57.570
I feel like it has
happened throughout my life

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:03.290
where you feel like
because of who you are,

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:05.190
you don't belong in that space.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:09.913
Or you look around you or
in my particular experience,

00:37:10.750 --> 00:37:13.020
I was always how they say cherry picked

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:16.440
to be in AP classes or honors classes.

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:20.560
And it was always like
a jarring experience

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:23.940
to look around you and
see only one other person

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:26.323
or two other people that look like you.

00:37:27.440 --> 00:37:29.253
And I feel like even an organizing.

00:37:29.253 --> 00:37:33.550
I remember when we first
started out IntegrateNYC,

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:35.539
we did this video project type of thing.

00:37:35.539 --> 00:37:40.539
And I started this work with
my white peer who also went

00:37:43.580 --> 00:37:44.890
to the same school as I did.

00:37:44.890 --> 00:37:47.170
And I remember we were both in the video.

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:49.960
And then when we came out of that,

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:51.070
we were standing together,

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:52.500
but people were going up to her

00:37:52.500 --> 00:37:55.720
and asking her questions
about what segregation means

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:57.070
and what integration mean

00:37:57.070 --> 00:37:59.200
and what integrating we see is about.

00:37:59.200 --> 00:38:01.053
And so, I feel like it's really,

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:03.650
it can be like we'll need to see

00:38:03.650 --> 00:38:08.190
how maybe people perceive you.

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:09.070
I don't know about lonely,

00:38:09.070 --> 00:38:10.480
but it's just exhausting, you know?

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:13.560
And like, although we're
activists and we're organizers,

00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:17.470
we can't constantly be
having the energy to have

00:38:17.470 --> 00:38:19.640
to stop and have a
conversation with someone

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:21.700
and try to change their whole perspective.

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:26.700
So I think like learning
how to build our boundaries,

00:38:26.930 --> 00:38:29.240
and like me, learning how
to build my boundaries

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:32.184
has been really important in not having

00:38:32.184 --> 00:38:35.119
those learning moments or
like imposter syndrome moments

00:38:35.119 --> 00:38:38.055
and knowing that I don't
have to be perfect.

00:38:38.055 --> 00:38:41.430
And also that my community's here with me.

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:51.530
- I'm still trying to formulate a response

00:38:51.530 --> 00:38:53.861
to that question because definitely

00:38:53.861 --> 00:38:56.763
a lot process and think about.

00:38:57.710 --> 00:39:00.110
And I feel like in my
organizing experience,

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:02.620
I didn't necessarily feel loneliness.

00:39:02.620 --> 00:39:04.580
Throughout my entire K
through 12 experience,

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:06.143
I've only really existed in spaces

00:39:06.143 --> 00:39:09.033
that were predominantly
Black and LatinX students.

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:12.690
So I definitely didn't feel
that sense of isolation

00:39:12.690 --> 00:39:15.240
in terms of my race or ethnicity

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:18.190
but I feel like a lot of what
I had to experience related

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:21.130
to organizing was definitely unpacking

00:39:21.130 --> 00:39:24.650
things like internalized
racism, internalized sexism

00:39:24.650 --> 00:39:26.230
and other traumas that come

00:39:26.230 --> 00:39:28.633
with being a member of a BIPOC group.

00:39:30.339 --> 00:39:32.420
And that in itself has been
extremely uncomfortable.

00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:35.190
And I remember when I first
started, Teens Take Charge

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:39.650
was often questioned my
commitment and questioning

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:43.160
whether or not this was a
mission that I could get behind.

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:44.950
But I realized that that process mostly

00:39:44.950 --> 00:39:48.610
is about kind of self-exploration
and self-discovery.

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:51.200
And it also definitely
connected to feelings

00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:53.190
of fear whenever I did publish things

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:55.580
or spoken public to people.

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:56.720
Because once you say something

00:39:56.720 --> 00:39:57.553
where it's out in the world,

00:39:57.553 --> 00:39:59.360
you can't necessarily take that back.

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:02.830
So like feeling, having
to feel like affirmed

00:40:02.830 --> 00:40:04.450
and competent in what I was saying

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:06.683
and believing in myself in terms of that.

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:09.450
But yeah, sorry if the
response was unclear,

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:11.510
I'm still trying to think about it.

00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:14.910
- I feel like that was so
powerful, what you just said.

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:17.455
Especially the piece about
not being able to take it back

00:40:17.455 --> 00:40:19.500
and taking that risk every time.

00:40:19.500 --> 00:40:22.870
So thank you for taking the
risk again here on this panel.

00:40:22.870 --> 00:40:24.250
I appreciate you.

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:25.350
Matt, I was wondering

00:40:25.350 --> 00:40:28.220
as these incredible
organizers were speaking,

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:31.140
what stood out to you the most?

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:33.760
And what's a question you have for them

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:37.780
or something that you think is
just really worth elevating?

00:40:37.780 --> 00:40:39.600
Because I feel like they said so much

00:40:39.600 --> 00:40:42.190
and I'd love to hear just
what's going on for you.

00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:45.330
- I mean, there's a dozen things.

00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:46.850
I mean, one of the first
ones is just thinking

00:40:46.850 --> 00:40:48.837
about how this doesn't
stop after high school.

00:40:48.837 --> 00:40:51.747
We're seeing all of you are in college.

00:40:51.747 --> 00:40:52.877
And I don't know where everyone's

00:40:52.877 --> 00:40:55.000
probably mostly a
primary way institutions.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:55.970
I'm at Dartmouth.

00:40:55.970 --> 00:40:58.430
I'm a full professor and encountered a lot

00:40:58.430 --> 00:41:00.360
of the same things that
you all are describing.

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:02.410
And Aneth, what you're talking about,

00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:03.410
sort of feeling like you're cherry pick

00:41:03.410 --> 00:41:05.350
to sort of be one of
the handful of people.

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:08.109
That's verbatim the experience
of most faculty of color

00:41:08.109 --> 00:41:10.910
at these institutions now.

00:41:10.910 --> 00:41:11.920
And I think when you sort of think

00:41:11.920 --> 00:41:14.000
about what it means to
sort of the live reality

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:15.400
of segregation means that
you're constantly encountering

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:18.410
people who are discovering
these things anew

00:41:18.410 --> 00:41:19.390
for the first time.

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:21.820
I think that's part of
the summer of 2020, right?

00:41:21.820 --> 00:41:24.770
For a lot of people, it
was eye opening experience

00:41:24.770 --> 00:41:27.130
about sort of depths of racism,
depths of police brutality.

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:28.190
And for a lot of communities color,

00:41:28.190 --> 00:41:29.713
it was another summer.

00:41:30.630 --> 00:41:32.210
And so I think that's
striking me the importance

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:33.210
of kind of trying to teach

00:41:33.210 --> 00:41:36.280
and bring these stories to the fore.

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:38.320
The thing I would love
to know more from you all

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:42.687
is there's a question in
the chat from Alex Apsahi.

00:41:45.501 --> 00:41:48.893
Tell me what teachers can do
to support teen activists.

00:41:48.893 --> 00:41:50.210
So in UIC, a lot of my own

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:51.890
students of color here on campus.

00:41:51.890 --> 00:41:52.787
So could you reflect on,

00:41:52.787 --> 00:41:54.800
first, think about
yourself as a high school,

00:41:54.800 --> 00:41:56.260
thing about yourselves now in college.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:59.117
What does it mean for when
you're working with teachers?

00:41:59.117 --> 00:42:00.940
What would you like to see teachers do

00:42:00.940 --> 00:42:01.970
in the classroom support to you,

00:42:01.970 --> 00:42:04.770
either in terms of how
they're structuring lessons,

00:42:04.770 --> 00:42:06.010
of teaching about
history is what histories

00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:07.630
would have helped you
to know when you were

00:42:07.630 --> 00:42:08.790
in junior high in high school.

00:42:08.790 --> 00:42:10.583
Or just in terms of
structuring conversations

00:42:10.583 --> 00:42:13.927
and making space for centering your voices

00:42:13.927 --> 00:42:15.178
and your experiences.

00:42:15.178 --> 00:42:17.613
I would love to learn
from you as an educator.

00:42:29.350 --> 00:42:30.763
- I guess I can go.

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:33.400
I mean, that's a loaded question.

00:42:33.400 --> 00:42:37.230
I guess, kind of one of
the best things that I have

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:40.230
when I was organizing in high school,

00:42:40.230 --> 00:42:42.900
there were definitely people in my school

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:47.600
that were not in full support
of everything that I was doing

00:42:47.600 --> 00:42:51.856
with my colleagues at Teens
Take Charge and IntegrateNYC.

00:42:51.856 --> 00:42:54.850
And that was probably one of
the most mentally taxing things

00:42:54.850 --> 00:42:59.850
to have to go through when you
are kind of in the authority

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:04.470
of somebody else who
clearly is not in support

00:43:04.470 --> 00:43:06.550
which was also just very
jarring for me to see

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:09.070
because like a lot of people mentioned,

00:43:09.070 --> 00:43:11.690
you're often in these primarily
Black and Brown schools

00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:16.173
where teachers who are bearing
the dominant identities.

00:43:17.759 --> 00:43:20.270
And I guess the biggest thing
that teachers did for me

00:43:20.270 --> 00:43:23.850
that were supporting me
were just settling time

00:43:23.850 --> 00:43:26.660
to talk and just talk through emotions,

00:43:26.660 --> 00:43:29.080
things that you're feeling,
checking in on you.

00:43:29.080 --> 00:43:32.080
And I think of course being lenient

00:43:32.080 --> 00:43:33.830
on like certain types of things

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:35.957
or your also emotional capacity

00:43:35.957 --> 00:43:38.142
and just passing on their wisdom as well.

00:43:38.142 --> 00:43:42.627
especially teachers of
color who have to also deal

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:49.070
with people who are above them
in a sense with their jobs

00:43:49.070 --> 00:43:52.440
but also have to manage their
own emotional kind of states

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:55.540
when they're working in
institutions like this.

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:58.990
And I guess the biggest
kind of thing that I can say

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:01.370
is just also be open to learn.

00:44:01.370 --> 00:44:05.277
I think a lot of places
that I have been a student

00:44:05.277 --> 00:44:07.915
and there's almost this kind
of teacher-student dynamic,

00:44:07.915 --> 00:44:11.520
where the teacher feels
like they just have

00:44:11.520 --> 00:44:15.880
to inculcate everything
they know into the student

00:44:15.880 --> 00:44:20.350
and you miss a lot of value
in the student's experience.

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:22.933
I think learning should
be a two-way street.

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:25.830
The teacher should also be the student

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:28.130
and the student should
also be the teacher.

00:44:28.130 --> 00:44:30.000
And honoring that you don't have to relate

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.133
to every single experience
your student has.

00:44:34.690 --> 00:44:36.913
And so kind of guiding that as well.

00:44:42.625 --> 00:44:43.540
- Thanks so much.

00:44:43.540 --> 00:44:45.520
I know that we're gonna now open it up

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:48.970
to our audience here.

00:44:48.970 --> 00:44:50.400
'Cause we have about hopefully,

00:44:50.400 --> 00:44:52.790
like a hundred students,
parents, organizers

00:44:52.790 --> 00:44:55.763
in the audience whose voices
we'd love to hear as well.

00:44:56.600 --> 00:44:58.510
And so I'd love to elevate

00:44:58.510 --> 00:45:00.803
some of their questions here.

00:45:03.568 --> 00:45:05.493
I believe, okay.

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:11.600
We have a question here,
which is specifically looking

00:45:13.640 --> 00:45:17.640
at white parents and school districts.

00:45:17.640 --> 00:45:20.340
I know that there's an
organization represented here.

00:45:20.340 --> 00:45:21.987
That's working to also
organize white parents.

00:45:21.987 --> 00:45:24.950
And I know Matt's book speaks specifically

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:26.670
about the resistance of white parents

00:45:26.670 --> 00:45:28.010
and y'all haven't really talked

00:45:28.010 --> 00:45:30.973
about the microaggressions
that you've experienced.

00:45:31.970 --> 00:45:35.580
What are some actions that
you think white parents

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:39.520
and white students can take
as allies in this work?

00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:43.650
If you were to talk about
what allyship looks like

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:45.290
and if allyship isn't enough,

00:45:45.290 --> 00:45:47.753
what being a co-conspirator it looks like.

00:46:00.860 --> 00:46:04.000
- I think this is also a
really interesting question.

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:06.810
I think, I was gonna say this

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:09.350
with the teacher questions
who I think the first,

00:46:09.350 --> 00:46:13.150
most important thing to do is to listen.

00:46:13.150 --> 00:46:15.910
We can sit here and tell
y'all our experiences

00:46:15.910 --> 00:46:17.720
and what our teachers might have done

00:46:17.720 --> 00:46:19.840
or what are white allies might have done

00:46:19.840 --> 00:46:21.830
but this comes from our community.

00:46:21.830 --> 00:46:25.190
So I think first thing is to really listen

00:46:25.190 --> 00:46:26.337
to what your community is saying

00:46:26.337 --> 00:46:29.500
and to what your young people are saying.

00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:31.593
Or the young people in your spaces.

00:46:33.580 --> 00:46:37.513
And then like, sort of be brave with that.

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:42.100
Especially if you hold like a position

00:46:42.100 --> 00:46:44.730
where you have institutional power

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:48.413
or any type of power where
people are listening to you

00:46:48.413 --> 00:46:50.490
because a lot of the times,

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:52.710
administration is not
listening to students.

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:55.230
So I think it's really
important to be brave

00:46:55.230 --> 00:46:56.063
with young people.

00:46:56.063 --> 00:47:00.760
A lot of time, I hear that young people

00:47:00.760 --> 00:47:03.700
are like organizers
because they're not afraid

00:47:03.700 --> 00:47:05.098
because we don't have anything to lose,

00:47:05.098 --> 00:47:06.760
but I think we're like,

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:10.270
we fight really hard because
we have everything to lose.

00:47:10.270 --> 00:47:12.913
So when I think like being brave with us.

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:16.903
Not just listening to us and
like making a space for us.

00:47:16.903 --> 00:47:20.369
I think something that one of my teachers

00:47:20.369 --> 00:47:23.448
in my school did and what
I wish the white parents

00:47:23.448 --> 00:47:27.007
and the white students in my school

00:47:27.007 --> 00:47:32.007
would have done is take, have
meetings with my principal.

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:35.890
I think one of the, like, yeah.

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:37.830
So my teacher had like,

00:47:37.830 --> 00:47:41.350
we all had a meeting with our principal.

00:47:41.350 --> 00:47:44.100
It was like the respects for
all coalition in my school

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:47.170
and my teacher, she's Ecuadorian like me.

00:47:47.170 --> 00:47:49.850
And she was the first
Ecuadorian and Latina teacher

00:47:49.850 --> 00:47:53.720
that I ever had in my educational career.

00:47:53.720 --> 00:47:56.920
And she just, she was brave for us.

00:47:56.920 --> 00:47:58.710
We said all of these things are missing

00:47:58.710 --> 00:48:02.180
and she voiced that to
us, to our principal.

00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:05.790
And like our principals didn't
even really have the words,

00:48:05.790 --> 00:48:10.290
he made excuses but that taking a stance

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:11.480
is really important.

00:48:11.480 --> 00:48:16.430
And taking up that space
that you know you can take up

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:19.870
to have a conversation that
we're saying needs to happen

00:48:19.870 --> 00:48:22.380
is really important as
sort of like taking,

00:48:22.380 --> 00:48:26.103
like giving back power to you
know it doesn't belong to you.

00:48:26.103 --> 00:48:29.143
Give back the power to the young people.

00:48:31.040 --> 00:48:34.700
- Yeah, I definitely wanna
underscore and that's point

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:37.280
about amplifying the
work that already exists

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:40.480
and work being done by primary
stakeholders of the issue.

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:43.410
And I think also it's really
important to make a distinction

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:46.560
between allyship and then
performative allyship.

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:48.600
And I personally define
performative allyship

00:48:48.600 --> 00:48:50.480
as people who kind of just engage

00:48:50.480 --> 00:48:51.740
within social justice work

00:48:51.740 --> 00:48:55.520
just for popularity or likes on Instagram.

00:48:55.520 --> 00:48:56.613
And I guess to combat that

00:48:56.613 --> 00:48:59.150
is definitely making
sure your interactions

00:48:59.150 --> 00:49:03.573
within organizing spaces are
meaningful and intentional.

00:49:04.472 --> 00:49:07.210
And you're kinda just really
engaged in what's happening

00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:10.720
and also committing and
kind of just being there

00:49:10.720 --> 00:49:13.960
at your full capacity at all times.

00:49:13.960 --> 00:49:15.803
So and wants to elaborate on that.

00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:19.000
- Thanks, Ayana.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:20.910
This is a question for
Matt from the audience.

00:49:20.910 --> 00:49:24.840
Could you please situate the
youth of color in the 1960s?

00:49:24.840 --> 00:49:27.650
Did they form independent
and distinct groups

00:49:27.650 --> 00:49:31.773
in this movement, even if
in partnerships with adults?

00:49:34.770 --> 00:49:38.595
- Yes, so, and there's multiple
different forms organizing,

00:49:38.595 --> 00:49:41.500
some kinda more clearly codified.

00:49:41.500 --> 00:49:44.050
So youth councils with the NAACP

00:49:44.050 --> 00:49:45.530
and with other organizations.

00:49:45.530 --> 00:49:47.630
And then there were more
kind of informal organizing

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:49.670
that would happen at the high school level

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:50.680
where people just kind of recognize

00:49:50.680 --> 00:49:52.010
that this was something that was,

00:49:52.010 --> 00:49:53.690
that they wanted to take action on.

00:49:53.690 --> 00:49:57.905
So it happened both sort
of just inter within youth

00:49:57.905 --> 00:50:00.011
and then more kind of intergenerational

00:50:00.011 --> 00:50:01.690
with parents as well.

00:50:01.690 --> 00:50:02.954
Sometimes organized around churches

00:50:02.954 --> 00:50:04.683
and other civic organizations.

00:50:05.895 --> 00:50:06.870
I think the important thing

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:11.870
was that it was consistent
ongoing organization

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:13.880
from young people.

00:50:13.880 --> 00:50:16.575
It was creative, it was relentless.

00:50:16.575 --> 00:50:18.430
Like a lot of the organizing today.

00:50:18.430 --> 00:50:21.067
And it was widespread across the city.

00:50:21.067 --> 00:50:23.890
So you don't get 460,000 students

00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:26.340
stay out of school without a
huge amount of coordination.

00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:31.340
And without people really
dedicated themselves to that work.

00:50:32.436 --> 00:50:33.900
So I think that those
are kinda connections

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:36.280
between then and now.

00:50:36.280 --> 00:50:38.330
- You know, I'd also
like to pop in here too

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:41.610
and add that what we
understand to be integration

00:50:41.610 --> 00:50:43.393
when we look retrospects,
perspectively back.

00:50:43.393 --> 00:50:46.130
And I'm not saying we don't wanna focus

00:50:46.130 --> 00:50:48.500
just on the movement of bodies, right?

00:50:48.500 --> 00:50:49.740
So something that we did

00:50:49.740 --> 00:50:52.160
the five point platform
on real integration,

00:50:52.160 --> 00:50:55.370
I'm sure anyone here who's
seen a pamphlet or wrote a book

00:50:55.370 --> 00:50:57.730
about the Black Panthers
or the Young Lords

00:50:57.730 --> 00:51:02.030
knows about a multipoint
platform by youth of color.

00:51:02.030 --> 00:51:04.820
We studied the Young Lords platforms.

00:51:04.820 --> 00:51:08.110
We study the Black Panthers
when we were doing the Five R.

00:51:08.110 --> 00:51:09.840
And so, I just wanna name that

00:51:09.840 --> 00:51:13.350
because when our students
were reclaiming integration

00:51:13.350 --> 00:51:16.150
and reclaiming what real integration was,

00:51:16.150 --> 00:51:18.120
it wasn't that they were making that up.

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:20.240
They were integrating youth movements

00:51:20.240 --> 00:51:21.920
over the course of time.

00:51:21.920 --> 00:51:23.410
And the Young Lords and the Black Panthers

00:51:23.410 --> 00:51:25.220
have been historically criminalized

00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:30.210
and situated as, I think not situated

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:32.220
in the same way as integration organizers,

00:51:32.220 --> 00:51:35.290
situated as some sort
of controversial groups

00:51:35.290 --> 00:51:38.400
and something that we've
tried to make very clear

00:51:38.400 --> 00:51:39.793
is that the Black Panthers

00:51:39.793 --> 00:51:42.170
and the Young Lords are as controversial

00:51:42.170 --> 00:51:43.990
as folks who were organizing

00:51:43.990 --> 00:51:46.140
for traditional forms of integration.

00:51:46.140 --> 00:51:48.132
There are benefits and disadvantages

00:51:48.132 --> 00:51:50.087
to all of the different platforms.

